WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.222
This is the executive
briefing for Book 09 of the

2
00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:06.950
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Network and Transport.

3
00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:12.323
It's written for the people who fund,
authorize and answer for the branch network:

4
00:00:12.323 --> 00:00:16.638
the CIO, the CISO, network program
executives and budget owners.

5
00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:19.712
It's built around decisions,
not configuration.

6
00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:24.300
For each one I'll say what has to be
decided, who owns it, and when it's needed.

7
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.675
Four questions organize the half hour.

8
00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:30.150
Why act now, when the target
architecture is years away.

9
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:34.488
What federal policy actually requires,
and where the money should go first.

10
00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:36.150
Who decides each step.

11
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.163
And how to proceed without turning
a faster network into a weaker one.

12
00:00:40.163 --> 00:00:45.269
The short version: no product shortens a
fiber path, so buy transport on physics,

13
00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:49.090
govern it as compliance, and
decide in the right order.

14
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.040
Here is where we're going.

15
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.803
Seven decisions, each with a named owner.

16
00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:58.153
None of them is a product choice, and
none of them requires an award to start.

17
00:00:58.153 --> 00:01:02.421
Several of them cost nothing but a
signature and a few weeks of staff time;

18
00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:06.546
the expensive mistakes come from
leaving them unmade while circuits,

19
00:01:06.546 --> 00:01:10.103
refreshes and migrations move
ahead on their own schedules.

20
00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:11.027
Here they are.

21
00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:15.665
One: the CISO decides that managed
TIC circuits end only when CISA

22
00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:20.053
confirms the replacement telemetry,
not when a branch cuts over.

23
00:01:20.053 --> 00:01:24.718
Two: the authorizing official records
today's gap as an accepted risk,

24
00:01:24.718 --> 00:01:30.202
with compensating measures and a funded
plan, so an assessor doesn't find it first.

25
00:01:30.202 --> 00:01:34.797
Three: security architecture
writes the target attributes

26
00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:39.391
into the edge-device refresh that
BOD 26-02 is forcing anyway.

27
00:01:39.391 --> 00:01:42.710
Four: the sponsor and CIO
set the investment order,

28
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.515
so staff time and requirements
work come before capital.

29
00:01:46.515 --> 00:01:50.039
Five: the same pair tests the
field-office refresh request

30
00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:54.077
against published capacity limits
before the money is committed.

31
00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:58.917
Six: they choose the network access
control platform, and tie the retirement

32
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:02.989
of the Windows RADIUS server to
the Entra migration, not to a date.

33
00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:07.990
Seven: the authorizing official and the
system owner hold the line on order.

34
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.527
The governor is live before traffic moves,

35
00:02:10.527 --> 00:02:14.152
and the boundary change is
decided before the first cutover.

36
00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:16.539
The thread running through all seven:

37
00:02:16.539 --> 00:02:19.927
policy names no product, but
it isn't requirement-free.

38
00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:22.828
A directive is already
spending the refresh money.

39
00:02:22.828 --> 00:02:25.627
And order and ownership
decide the outcome.

40
00:02:25.627 --> 00:02:29.875
The rest of the briefing gives
the evidence behind each one,

41
00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:32.500
in the order why, what, who and how.

42
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.275
Why act at all?

43
00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:37.313
Start with the two decisions
programs keep merging.

44
00:02:37.313 --> 00:02:39.712
Choosing a pipe is a physics decision.

45
00:02:39.712 --> 00:02:44.301
Light in fiber covers about one
kilometer every five microseconds.

46
00:02:44.301 --> 00:02:47.275
New York to London has
a round-trip floor of 56

47
00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:50.813
milliseconds before any
equipment touches a packet.

48
00:02:50.813 --> 00:02:55.766
The well-known 150-millisecond voice
budget is mouth to ear, and the phones

49
00:02:55.766 --> 00:03:00.638
themselves take a large share, which
leaves the network roughly 70 to 100.

50
00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:04.669
On the book's worked arithmetic, a
Seattle office hairpinning through

51
00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:08.700
northern Virginia spends about 60
milliseconds one way on geography.

52
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.250
That's most of the allowance, gone
before the call reaches anything useful.

53
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.087
No product moves that floor.

54
00:03:15.087 --> 00:03:18.988
Local breakout fixes the
geographic part and nothing else.

55
00:03:18.988 --> 00:03:22.238
Choosing what governs the pipe
is a compliance decision.

56
00:03:22.238 --> 00:03:24.973
Direct internet access solves the hairpin,

57
00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:28.088
and it satisfies none of
the protection controls.

58
00:03:28.088 --> 00:03:32.478
Boundary protection, flow enforcement,
transmission confidentiality,

59
00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:36.576
malware inspection and monitoring
all come from the control layer.

60
00:03:36.576 --> 00:03:42.538
Transport selection closes exactly one
control family: telecommunications diversity.

61
00:03:42.538 --> 00:03:45.400
So here's the risk an
executive should watch for.

62
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.075
A branch moved from a governed
hairpin to bare local breakout has

63
00:03:49.075 --> 00:03:52.750
lost assessed boundary protection
relative to where it started.

64
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.800
The book's phrase for that is a compliance
regression with better latency.

65
00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.558
It looks like modernization
on a dashboard,

66
00:03:59.558 --> 00:04:02.162
and it reads as a
finding in an assessment.

67
00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:05.883
Every decision in this briefing
keeps those two choices separate:

68
00:04:05.883 --> 00:04:07.709
buy the pipe on measured physics,

69
00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:12.038
and take the protection from the governor
and the security services above it.

70
00:04:12.038 --> 00:04:16.297
Each new pipe also adds a managed
interface someone has to govern,

71
00:04:16.297 --> 00:04:21.070
so more circuits without more control
means more exposure, not less.

72
00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.933
The second reason to act is that the
external clocks are already running,

73
00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:28.333
whether or not a network
program is funded.

74
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:29.945
Read the table from the top.

75
00:04:29.945 --> 00:04:36.807
All FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to
NIST's historical list on September 22.

76
00:04:36.807 --> 00:04:39.528
That isn't a ban on existing modules,

77
00:04:39.528 --> 00:04:43.657
but new purchases should
state the 140-3 expectation.

78
00:04:43.657 --> 00:04:50.342
OMB M-26-15, the post-quantum memo, wanted
agency migration plans by October 22,

79
00:04:50.342 --> 00:04:54.798
2026, and it says anything
terminating TLS or IPsec,

80
00:04:54.798 --> 00:05:02.119
firewalls and VPN concentrators included,
must support post-quantum key exchange.

81
00:05:02.119 --> 00:05:08.137
OMB M-26-14 rescinded the old logging
memo; the first agency logging

82
00:05:08.137 --> 00:05:14.483
plan is due November 18, with maturity
milestones running to July 2027.

83
00:05:14.483 --> 00:05:19.495
BOD 26-04's third phase lands December 7.

84
00:05:19.495 --> 00:05:24.445
FedRAMP's 2026 rules become
mandatory on January 1.

85
00:05:24.445 --> 00:05:30.495
And the GSA EIS first option
period ends July 31, 2027.

86
00:05:30.495 --> 00:05:33.057
The row in red is the
one that spends money.

87
00:05:33.057 --> 00:05:39.135
BOD 26-02 requires all identified
end-of-support edge devices off agency

88
00:05:39.135 --> 00:05:45.719
networks by August 5, 2027, with a first
decommissioning milestone in February.

89
00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:47.863
I'll come back to it,
because it's the fork

90
00:05:47.863 --> 00:05:50.408
in the road for the whole
branch architecture.

91
00:05:50.408 --> 00:05:52.733
The panel on the right is the honest part.

92
00:05:52.733 --> 00:05:57.178
Three of these requirements fall inside
roughly fifteen months, and all of them

93
00:05:57.178 --> 00:06:01.421
draw on the same network and security
staff a branch-edge program would need.

94
00:06:01.421 --> 00:06:04.483
An agency promising an
SD-WAN program next year

95
00:06:04.483 --> 00:06:07.782
alongside them is promising
the same people twice.

96
00:06:07.782 --> 00:06:10.908
The book's advice is to say
that plainly in the plan.

97
00:06:10.908 --> 00:06:16.745
One clock has already run out: the legacy
telecom contracts all ended by May 31,

98
00:06:16.745 --> 00:06:20.758
2026, so the architecture is
now a task-order question,

99
00:06:20.758 --> 00:06:23.580
not a contract-vehicle question.

100
00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.505
Not acting doesn't mean standing still.

101
00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:28.842
It means three things happen by default.

102
00:06:28.842 --> 00:06:32.005
First, money gets spent
without a choice being made.

103
00:06:32.005 --> 00:06:37.117
The BOD 26-02 refresh will
happen on a directive clock.

104
00:06:37.117 --> 00:06:39.018
Run as like-for-like replacement,

105
00:06:39.018 --> 00:06:43.734
the same device class with the same absence
of policy and telemetry, it spends the

106
00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:46.338
money and closes the architecture question

107
00:06:46.338 --> 00:06:48.731
for the service life of the new equipment.

108
00:06:48.731 --> 00:06:50.918
Deferral also has a measured price.

109
00:06:50.918 --> 00:06:57.906
GAO reported in August 2026 that billing
for six agencies' legacy Networx contracts

110
00:06:57.906 --> 00:07:02.299
rose from about 13.4 million
dollars in February 2025

111
00:07:02.299 --> 00:07:06.592
to about 40.9 million in
March, a 206 percent increase.

112
00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:11.004
The book calls that the strongest
cost-of-delay number in the federal record.

113
00:07:11.004 --> 00:07:15.193
Second, gaps surface as findings
rather than accepted risks.

114
00:07:15.193 --> 00:07:20.318
The technical situation is identical either
way; the institutional position isn't.

115
00:07:20.318 --> 00:07:25.591
And cutting managed TIC circuits before
CISA confirms replacement telemetry

116
00:07:25.591 --> 00:07:30.156
trades a gap in transport for a gap
in CISA's visibility, which is worse.

117
00:07:30.156 --> 00:07:32.693
Third, cost gets blamed on the cloud.

118
00:07:32.693 --> 00:07:36.763
When workloads migrate before
transport is governed and measured,

119
00:07:36.763 --> 00:07:40.179
slowness is attributed to the
only thing being measured.

120
00:07:40.179 --> 00:07:44.742
The remedies, more instances and
more caching, raise cloud spend.

121
00:07:44.742 --> 00:07:48.633
The book is careful to label
that pattern its own inference,

122
00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:53.555
built on documented parts, so treat it
as a hypothesis to test in your estate.

123
00:07:53.555 --> 00:07:55.605
And one risk sits under all of them.

124
00:07:55.605 --> 00:07:57.692
The governor is attack surface.

125
00:07:57.692 --> 00:08:03.782
In February 2026, Emergency Directive 26-03
ordered action on Cisco SD-WAN systems

126
00:08:03.782 --> 00:08:09.604
after actors were found compromising
them and keeping long-term persistence.

127
00:08:09.604 --> 00:08:13.200
Whatever you buy, protect it
before it carries traffic.

128
00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.400
What does TIC 3.0 actually require?

129
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.518
The book reads it from
the current documents

130
00:08:18.518 --> 00:08:20.350
rather than from what's said about them.

131
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:24.350
CISA republished the whole
set in September 2026.

132
00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:29.847
The revision tables say the only 2026
changes were updated footnote links,

133
00:08:29.847 --> 00:08:35.788
so the substance dates from July 2025, and
April 2021 for the Traditional use case.

134
00:08:35.788 --> 00:08:39.414
One practical note for
anyone writing a package:

135
00:08:39.414 --> 00:08:44.400
CISA's landing page labels three
volumes 1.1, but the covers read 1.2.

136
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.462
Cite the cover.

137
00:08:45.462 --> 00:08:50.675
There are four use cases: Traditional,
Branch Office, Remote User and Cloud.

138
00:08:50.675 --> 00:08:52.637
There is no SASE use case.

139
00:08:52.637 --> 00:08:55.923
An agency deploying SASE
at its branches operates

140
00:08:55.923 --> 00:08:58.674
under the Branch Office
and Cloud use cases.

141
00:08:58.674 --> 00:09:02.568
The program guidebook
calls TIC 3.0 descriptive,

142
00:09:02.568 --> 00:09:06.613
not prescriptive, and the catalog
says it sets goals, not methods.

143
00:09:06.613 --> 00:09:08.962
But two statements constrain a branch.

144
00:09:08.962 --> 00:09:13.353
Policy enforcement parity: once
you decide a capability is needed,

145
00:09:13.353 --> 00:09:16.467
you apply it on every
path, and the Branch Office

146
00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:19.900
use case names SD-WAN paths
as one place that applies.

147
00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:25.613
And preserve CISA's visibility as branches
connect directly to external services.

148
00:09:25.613 --> 00:09:31.229
Discretion also carries an obligation: where
TIC controls don't cover residual risk,

149
00:09:31.229 --> 00:09:35.000
agencies must apply controls
or compensating controls.

150
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:40.075
CISA's June 2026 SASE guide
states the consequence plainly.

151
00:09:40.075 --> 00:09:45.306
TIC 3.0 doesn't require routing
traffic through TIC access points if

152
00:09:45.306 --> 00:09:49.913
an alternative gives both the agency
and CISA situational awareness.

153
00:09:49.913 --> 00:09:52.413
That guide permits and describes SASE.

154
00:09:52.413 --> 00:09:53.838
It doesn't require it.

155
00:09:53.838 --> 00:09:56.730
So when someone says TIC requires SD-WAN,

156
00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:01.200
or that TIC forbids local breakout,
both claims fail the check.

157
00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.479
The honest position is narrower
and stronger: TIC sets goals,

158
00:10:05.479 --> 00:10:09.074
local breakout is allowed,
and the agency must show

159
00:10:09.074 --> 00:10:13.290
parity and preserve CISA's
view on every path it opens.

160
00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:16.483
The opposite mistake is just as common:

161
00:10:16.483 --> 00:10:21.827
concluding that because policy is
technology-neutral, the branch is unconstrained.

162
00:10:21.827 --> 00:10:22.653
It isn't.

163
00:10:22.653 --> 00:10:26.170
Four binding obligations land
directly on branch equipment,

164
00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:27.964
and none of them picks a product.

165
00:10:27.964 --> 00:10:33.085
Egress DNS must go to CISA's Protective
DNS; that's statutory, and a vendor's

166
00:10:33.085 --> 00:10:38.540
own resolver in its place satisfies the
product and breaches the requirement.

167
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:43.432
BOD 23-02 requires internet-exposed
management interfaces to be

168
00:10:43.432 --> 00:10:48.415
taken off the internet, or fronted
by a separate enforcement point.

169
00:10:48.415 --> 00:10:56.690
BOD 26-02 takes end-of-support edge devices
off agency networks by August 5, 2027.

170
00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:00.886
And M-26-15 requires
post-quantum key exchange

171
00:11:00.886 --> 00:11:04.640
on anything that terminates TLS or IPsec.

172
00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:09.377
The obligations that bite hardest are
often the ones an agency wrote itself.

173
00:11:09.377 --> 00:11:12.966
Once a branch breaks out locally,
it's an external boundary,

174
00:11:12.966 --> 00:11:16.628
and the system security plan
asserts boundary protection,

175
00:11:16.628 --> 00:11:20.440
traffic flow policy, monitoring
and alternate paths about it.

176
00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.878
The gap surfaces at assessment
time, not deployment time.

177
00:11:23.878 --> 00:11:26.990
That's an evidence argument,
not a mandate argument.

178
00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.665
The right-hand panel
matters to budget owners.

179
00:11:29.665 --> 00:11:33.190
No federal instrument
requires SD-WAN or SASE.

180
00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:38.134
No instrument requires the WAN itself to
be encrypted where the payload is already

181
00:11:38.134 --> 00:11:41.931
protected end to end, so an overlay
is a defensible risk decision,

182
00:11:41.931 --> 00:11:46.015
and the book says it shouldn't be
budgeted as a compliance obligation.

183
00:11:46.015 --> 00:11:51.153
And FedRAMP certifies cloud services, not
the routes an agency uses to reach them.

184
00:11:51.153 --> 00:11:54.567
When a network change is
refused on FedRAMP grounds,

185
00:11:54.567 --> 00:11:57.665
ask one question: which
control does it violate?

186
00:11:57.665 --> 00:12:01.345
A refusal with no control
basis is a governance failure,

187
00:12:01.345 --> 00:12:03.630
and should be recorded as one.

188
00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.280
Logging and CISA
telemetry are where branch

189
00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.180
modernization most often
goes wrong quietly.

190
00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:18.817
OMB M-26-14 rescinded
M-21-31 on May 22, 2026.

191
00:12:18.817 --> 00:12:22.941
Logs must be searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year,

192
00:12:22.941 --> 00:12:26.683
and among the minimum content,
agencies must be able to show

193
00:12:26.683 --> 00:12:30.806
source and destination addresses,
ports and session attributes.

194
00:12:30.806 --> 00:12:35.480
That's the transport-layer record a branch
with nothing at its edge doesn't produce.

195
00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.055
The first agency logging
plan is due November 18.

196
00:12:39.055 --> 00:12:42.041
When branches leave the
managed TIC service,

197
00:12:42.041 --> 00:12:45.592
the sensor telemetry CISA
has been receiving stops.

198
00:12:45.592 --> 00:12:48.593
CISA's SASE guide says so in plain terms.

199
00:12:48.593 --> 00:12:53.224
The replacement is CLAW, CISA's
Comprehensive Log Aggregation Warehouse,

200
00:12:53.224 --> 00:12:57.781
and onboarding needs the agency, CISA
and the provider working together.

201
00:12:57.781 --> 00:13:01.080
That's a three-party dependency
with its own lead time,

202
00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.005
not a configuration change
one team completes.

203
00:13:04.005 --> 00:13:08.115
It belongs on the critical path
beside the authorization package,

204
00:13:08.115 --> 00:13:10.880
and the field-office case
starts it in Phase 0.

205
00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.180
Here is the decision.

206
00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:16.930
The exit criterion for leaving MTIPS,
and for ending each branch's MPLS,

207
00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:20.780
is confirmed CLAW parity, not
branch cutover completion.

208
00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:24.845
Cutting circuits first trades a
transport gap for a visibility gap,

209
00:13:24.845 --> 00:13:27.530
which is worse than the
position being fixed.

210
00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.267
Ownership splits three ways on purpose.

211
00:13:30.267 --> 00:13:34.181
The CISO decides CLAW onboarding
and when parity is reached.

212
00:13:34.181 --> 00:13:38.305
Security operations does the
confirming, branch by branch.

213
00:13:38.305 --> 00:13:42.851
And under the Federal Acquisition
Regulation only a contracting officer can

214
00:13:42.851 --> 00:13:46.697
terminate a contract, so the
contracting officer ends the circuit,

215
00:13:46.697 --> 00:13:48.655
and only after that confirmation.

216
00:13:48.655 --> 00:13:53.010
No engineer can cancel a circuit,
however redundant it looks.

217
00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.935
For many agencies, neither SD-WAN
nor a new TIC use case is close.

218
00:13:58.935 --> 00:14:02.573
The acquisition isn't funded and
the task orders aren't placed.

219
00:14:02.573 --> 00:14:06.462
The book calls that interval the
long interim, and it argues the

220
00:14:06.462 --> 00:14:11.398
architecture by elimination rather than
by mandate, because no mandate exists.

221
00:14:11.398 --> 00:14:16.898
The legacy telecom vehicles
all ended by May 31, 2026.

222
00:14:16.898 --> 00:14:20.762
The hairpin is still permitted,
but CISA's own guidebook says it

223
00:14:20.762 --> 00:14:25.136
raised the cost and reduced the
effectiveness of TIC for cloud services.

224
00:14:25.136 --> 00:14:27.519
A new agency-operated access point is a

225
00:14:27.519 --> 00:14:30.573
capital and round-the-clock
staffing commitment.

226
00:14:30.573 --> 00:14:33.772
VPN backhaul is a hairpin
under another name.

227
00:14:33.772 --> 00:14:39.097
And Microsoft no longer recommends
ExpressRoute for Microsoft 365.

228
00:14:39.097 --> 00:14:43.349
What's left is local breakout, a
cloud-delivered security stack at a nearby

229
00:14:43.349 --> 00:14:47.948
point of presence, and a policy layer at
the branch edge deciding what goes where.

230
00:14:47.948 --> 00:14:50.985
The target is years away
for structural reasons.

231
00:14:50.985 --> 00:14:52.910
Acquisition takes quarters.

232
00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:56.561
Agencies send budget
submissions to OMB in September,

233
00:14:56.561 --> 00:15:01.023
so a program conceived now has
usually missed the next budget year.

234
00:15:01.023 --> 00:15:05.147
A thousand branches is a multi-year
deployment on logistics alone.

235
00:15:05.147 --> 00:15:08.973
And every new device class at
every branch is assessment work.

236
00:15:08.973 --> 00:15:12.119
But the interval isn't empty:
four of the six functions a

237
00:15:12.119 --> 00:15:16.035
branch-edge layer provides are
partly obtainable before any award.

238
00:15:16.035 --> 00:15:19.785
The decision on this slide belongs
to the authorizing official.

239
00:15:19.785 --> 00:15:24.281
Record in the risk register and the
affected packages which control assertions

240
00:15:24.281 --> 00:15:26.965
depend on transport evidence
that doesn't exist,

241
00:15:26.965 --> 00:15:31.260
the compensating measures, and the
remediation with its funding dependency.

242
00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:35.135
An undocumented gap found by
an assessor is a finding.

243
00:15:35.135 --> 00:15:38.548
A documented gap with a
plan is an accepted risk.

244
00:15:38.548 --> 00:15:41.220
Do it now, while the gap exists.

245
00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:44.920
This is the most important
planning item in the book.

246
00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:53.545
BOD 26-02, issued February 5, 2026, puts
branch edge hardware on a directive clock.

247
00:15:53.545 --> 00:15:55.408
Inventory came due in May.

248
00:15:55.408 --> 00:15:59.720
Devices whose support ends by
February 2027 come out then.

249
00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.333
All identified end-of-support edge devices

250
00:16:03.333 --> 00:16:06.945
must be off agency networks
by August 5, 2027.

251
00:16:06.945 --> 00:16:09.870
After that, discovery is continuous.

252
00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:14.322
The directive covers devices on the
network boundary that are reachable from

253
00:16:14.322 --> 00:16:18.432
the internet, so a branch router with
its own internet circuit qualifies.

254
00:16:18.432 --> 00:16:20.622
An interior access switch may not,

255
00:16:20.622 --> 00:16:23.983
so don't assume the directive
funds the access layer.

256
00:16:23.983 --> 00:16:26.642
For an agency with aging branch equipment,

257
00:16:26.642 --> 00:16:31.051
part of the capital it assumed it didn't
have is going to be spent anyway,

258
00:16:31.051 --> 00:16:35.232
on a compliance basis, inside the
window everyone calls years away.

259
00:16:35.232 --> 00:16:39.957
And a refresh is exactly the moment a
branch architecture gets chosen again.

260
00:16:39.957 --> 00:16:43.160
The default choice is like-for-like,
which spends the money

261
00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:46.296
and closes the question for
the life of the new equipment.

262
00:16:46.296 --> 00:16:49.020
The recommendation is narrow and cheap.

263
00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:53.032
Tell whoever is scoping the
replacements, in writing,

264
00:16:53.032 --> 00:16:56.953
what the eventual target
needs from a branch device:

265
00:16:56.953 --> 00:17:02.515
application-aware steering and path
measurement, deterministic egress,

266
00:17:02.515 --> 00:17:06.527
per-application telemetry
the log pipeline can take,

267
00:17:06.527 --> 00:17:09.445
post-quantum key exchange under M-26-15,

268
00:17:09.445 --> 00:17:15.645
a management plane that satisfies BOD 23-02,
and an explicit FIPS 140-3 expectation.

269
00:17:15.645 --> 00:17:18.305
Security architecture owns that decision,

270
00:17:18.305 --> 00:17:23.033
because the refresh row in the book's
matrix fixes the branch design for years.

271
00:17:23.033 --> 00:17:25.657
Acquisition writes it into every order.

272
00:17:25.657 --> 00:17:28.244
It's needed before the
replacements are scoped.

273
00:17:28.244 --> 00:17:30.372
It costs requirements-writing time,

274
00:17:30.372 --> 00:17:34.200
and not doing it spends the same
money and closes the option.

275
00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.588
So where should the money
go, and in what order?

276
00:17:37.588 --> 00:17:42.575
The book's chapters line up into five
tiers, cheapest and most certain first.

277
00:17:42.575 --> 00:17:45.363
One: staff time, with no award.

278
00:17:45.363 --> 00:17:50.551
Write the application-to-path policy
that SC-7(4) already asks for.

279
00:17:50.551 --> 00:17:52.762
Instrument a sample of branches.

280
00:17:52.762 --> 00:17:56.651
Fix deterministic egress,
usually a NAT decision.

281
00:17:56.651 --> 00:18:00.275
Register the cleartext
protocols crossing branch links.

282
00:18:00.275 --> 00:18:05.800
And test, not just read, that DNS
reaches CISA's Protective DNS.

283
00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:07.475
Two: requirements time.

284
00:18:07.475 --> 00:18:13.012
Put the target attributes
into the BOD 26-02 refresh.

285
00:18:13.012 --> 00:18:15.912
Three: accreditation and CLAW onboarding.

286
00:18:15.912 --> 00:18:20.759
The field-office case calls accreditation,
not procurement, the critical path;

287
00:18:20.759 --> 00:18:22.921
circuits arrive in about a quarter,

288
00:18:22.921 --> 00:18:26.351
an authorized breakout
architecture takes far longer.

289
00:18:26.351 --> 00:18:28.312
Four: protect the governor.

290
00:18:28.312 --> 00:18:35.114
Phishing-resistant MFA, a hardened baseline,
patching inside BOD 26-04's windows,

291
00:18:35.114 --> 00:18:40.163
the shortest of which is three
days, and logs held off the box.

292
00:18:40.163 --> 00:18:42.775
Five, and only then: capital.

293
00:18:42.775 --> 00:18:46.606
Hubs first, then office waves
timed to MPLS contract renewals,

294
00:18:46.606 --> 00:18:51.113
scoped to the branches and applications
where measurement says it pays.

295
00:18:51.113 --> 00:18:53.425
The panel on the right is about pricing.

296
00:18:53.425 --> 00:18:58.876
Moving from MPLS to internet access plus
a cloud security service changes the cost

297
00:18:58.876 --> 00:19:04.011
variable from site count and port speed
to user count, and the per-user security

298
00:19:04.011 --> 00:19:09.225
subscription is the line most likely to
dominate, and the one most often left out.

299
00:19:09.225 --> 00:19:14.988
Price your own circuits in GSA's EIS pricer
rather than citing a marketing multiple.

300
00:19:14.988 --> 00:19:18.034
And don't justify the
program on latency alone;

301
00:19:18.034 --> 00:19:21.400
the stronger argument is
governance and evidence.

302
00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:24.632
The sponsor and CIO set
scope, funding and sequence,

303
00:19:24.632 --> 00:19:30.060
and this order needs to be settled before
the next September budget submission.

304
00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:34.448
Field offices are where these
decisions become a budget line.

305
00:19:34.448 --> 00:19:38.995
Chapter 11 works a case many
civilian agencies will recognize:

306
00:19:38.995 --> 00:19:44.328
about 1,500 field offices, about 70,000
endpoints, and a request for about 50

307
00:19:44.328 --> 00:19:47.563
million dollars to refresh a Configuration

308
00:19:47.563 --> 00:19:50.798
Manager distribution
point in every office.

309
00:19:50.798 --> 00:19:51.998
Three findings.

310
00:19:51.998 --> 00:19:54.835
First, the unit cost doesn't reconcile.

311
00:19:54.835 --> 00:20:00.091
Fifty million over 1,500
offices is 33,333 dollars each,

312
00:20:00.091 --> 00:20:04.647
about twice the book's
fully loaded server case.

313
00:20:04.647 --> 00:20:08.595
So the sponsor needs to say whether
that figure is acquisition cost

314
00:20:08.595 --> 00:20:12.260
or five-year cost of ownership,
and itemize anything bundled in.

315
00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:13.798
Second, capacity.

316
00:20:13.798 --> 00:20:19.000
Microsoft's published limit is 4,000
clients per distribution point,

317
00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:22.560
so the estate needs 24, two
at each of 12 hub sites.

318
00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:28.560
The other 1,476 exist only to
compensate for slow branch circuits.

319
00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:29.985
Third, timing.

320
00:20:29.985 --> 00:20:35.288
Microsoft has moved Configuration Manager
to an annual release cadence and says new

321
00:20:35.288 --> 00:20:38.350
device-management innovation
happens in Intune,

322
00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:41.785
so hardware bought now
depreciates past its likely use.

323
00:20:41.785 --> 00:20:46.615
The alternative fixes the circuit:
internet access sized to seats,

324
00:20:46.615 --> 00:20:51.885
a 5G continuity path, and an SD-WAN
overlay, with breakout at the hubs first.

325
00:20:51.885 --> 00:20:55.838
On the book's labeled planning
assumptions, that needs about 10 million

326
00:20:55.838 --> 00:21:00.060
dollars of first-year capital and turns
cumulatively positive in year four.

327
00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:04.372
But the financial case turns on one
number the analysis doesn't hold.

328
00:21:04.372 --> 00:21:10.122
Breakeven is 873 dollars of current
MPLS cost per office per month.

329
00:21:10.122 --> 00:21:12.704
Above it the program pays for itself;

330
00:21:12.704 --> 00:21:16.060
below it, argue capability
rather than savings.

331
00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:21.035
So the decision for the sponsor and CIO
is to decompose the request and validate

332
00:21:21.035 --> 00:21:26.520
that MPLS figure before any comparison,
and before the refresh money is committed.

333
00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:30.495
Network access control looks
like an engineering topic.

334
00:21:30.495 --> 00:21:33.732
It reaches the executive agenda
because it's usually the

335
00:21:33.732 --> 00:21:37.245
last piece of the network still
standing on Active Directory.

336
00:21:37.245 --> 00:21:41.520
Windows Network Policy Server, the
RADIUS server built into Windows,

337
00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:45.570
authorizes devices against Active
Directory computer objects.

338
00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:50.195
Microsoft's Entra join guidance says
Entra joined devices don't support that.

339
00:21:50.195 --> 00:21:54.357
There's no Entra RADIUS service,
and NPS has no posture engine.

340
00:21:54.357 --> 00:21:58.957
So as the Entra migration
progresses, NPS becomes less useful.

341
00:21:58.957 --> 00:22:02.917
And if NPS goes away before
its replacement is validated,

342
00:22:02.917 --> 00:22:07.970
the result isn't a degraded experience;
it's a total loss of network access.

343
00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:11.170
The older password methods are
breaking on their own schedule.

344
00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:16.378
Credential Guard, on by default
on eligible Windows 11 devices,

345
00:22:16.378 --> 00:22:20.685
blocks single sign-on for
PEAP-MSCHAPv2 Wi-Fi and VPN,

346
00:22:20.685 --> 00:22:27.295
and the October 2026 Windows update
restricts NTLMv1-derived credentials too.

347
00:22:27.295 --> 00:22:31.645
Agencies that followed older guidance
now have a deadline they didn't choose.

348
00:22:31.645 --> 00:22:36.948
The program's target is certificate-based
EAP-TLS for every managed device,

349
00:22:36.948 --> 00:22:41.824
with posture read from Intune's
compliance service by a NAC platform,

350
00:22:41.824 --> 00:22:46.870
rolled out in four phases over roughly
90 to 180 days, monitor mode first.

351
00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:50.962
One caution for cloud-delivered
RADIUS: as of October 21, 2026,

352
00:22:50.962 --> 00:22:56.281
several well-known cloud RADIUS offerings
have no FedRAMP listing, so confirm

353
00:22:56.281 --> 00:23:01.845
the specific function is inside a certified
offering before designing around it.

354
00:23:01.845 --> 00:23:05.139
The decision sits with
the sponsor and CIO:

355
00:23:05.139 --> 00:23:08.245
which NAC platform, and when NPS retires.

356
00:23:08.245 --> 00:23:12.712
The book's rule is that the retirement
is triggered by the Entra migration,

357
00:23:12.712 --> 00:23:16.080
not by a date, and the replacement
must be in place before

358
00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:20.108
cloud-native cutover retires the
computer objects NPS depends on.

359
00:23:20.108 --> 00:23:22.840
The CISO sets the posture requirement.

360
00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:24.778
Who owns all this?

361
00:23:24.778 --> 00:23:27.540
The book's sharpest
observation is that the most

362
00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.716
expensive network mistakes
aren't engineering errors.

363
00:23:30.716 --> 00:23:35.138
They're ownership gaps: a circuit bought
before anyone owned the governor,

364
00:23:35.138 --> 00:23:38.183
a cutover before anyone
filed the boundary change,

365
00:23:38.183 --> 00:23:42.678
a telemetry contract cancelled before
anyone confirmed its replacement.

366
00:23:42.678 --> 00:23:46.502
Naming one decider for each step
is cheaper than any product.

367
00:23:46.502 --> 00:23:48.977
The register separates
deciding from doing.

368
00:23:48.977 --> 00:23:52.166
A role decides when it is
accountable and can say no.

369
00:23:52.166 --> 00:23:54.779
Network engineering does most of the work,

370
00:23:54.779 --> 00:23:57.915
but decides only physics
and enforcement phasing.

371
00:23:57.915 --> 00:23:59.427
Read down the middle column.

372
00:23:59.427 --> 00:24:04.818
The sponsor and CIO decide the governor
platforms and the order of branch waves,

373
00:24:04.818 --> 00:24:08.166
because those fix cost and
the boundary for years;

374
00:24:08.166 --> 00:24:12.903
acquisition is engaged in Phase 0
and waves follow contract renewals.

375
00:24:12.903 --> 00:24:16.677
The CISO sets the security
envelope every order carries.

376
00:24:16.677 --> 00:24:21.812
Security architecture decides the refresh
attributes and where each enforcement

377
00:24:21.812 --> 00:24:26.065
capability sits, but doesn't accept
the risk of a non-conformant design.

378
00:24:26.065 --> 00:24:31.484
The authorizing official accepts risk, so
four rows land there: the boundary change,

379
00:24:31.484 --> 00:24:36.981
decided before the first production cutover;
the Microsoft 365 Optimize exception,

380
00:24:36.981 --> 00:24:40.830
where device-side tenant
restrictions are a precondition;

381
00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.678
the interim gap; and any standing
exception with no end date.

382
00:24:44.678 --> 00:24:46.440
Two rows split on purpose.

383
00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:51.553
The authorizing official decides whether
the boundary may change, but the system

384
00:24:51.553 --> 00:24:56.515
owner decides, site by site, whether that
site's control layer is live and logging.

385
00:24:56.515 --> 00:24:59.964
And telemetry splits three
ways: the CISO decides,

386
00:24:59.964 --> 00:25:04.890
security operations confirms, and
the contracting officer terminates.

387
00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:08.820
When an architectural refusal
arrives with no control basis,

388
00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:13.180
record it as a governance failure,
with the refusing role named.

389
00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:16.093
How to proceed comes down to order.

390
00:25:16.093 --> 00:25:20.530
Order decides whether branch
modernization is a gain or a regression.

391
00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:22.842
Step zero: secure the governor.

392
00:25:22.842 --> 00:25:27.595
The SD-WAN controller and its edges are
internet-facing with estate-wide authority,

393
00:25:27.595 --> 00:25:30.212
so they come under
vulnerability management,

394
00:25:30.212 --> 00:25:33.931
privileged access and off-box
logging before they carry traffic.

395
00:25:33.931 --> 00:25:36.701
Step one: buy the pipe
on measured physics,

396
00:25:36.701 --> 00:25:40.205
latency and loss recorded
per site before the order.

397
00:25:40.205 --> 00:25:43.942
Step two: deploy the governor
before production traffic;

398
00:25:43.942 --> 00:25:48.793
a site whose control layer isn't live
and logging doesn't get its new egress.

399
00:25:48.793 --> 00:25:53.121
Step three: treat the boundary change
as the significant change it is,

400
00:25:53.121 --> 00:25:56.006
decided before the first
production cutover.

401
00:25:56.006 --> 00:26:02.268
Step four: handle Microsoft 365 Optimize
traffic through its own signed exception.

402
00:26:02.268 --> 00:26:04.541
Step five: register the evidence,

403
00:26:04.541 --> 00:26:09.255
and confirm CISA telemetry parity
before any legacy circuit ends.

404
00:26:09.255 --> 00:26:14.768
The book is candid that no memo, directive
or executive order sets this sequence.

405
00:26:14.768 --> 00:26:19.618
It's an engineering dependency, and saying
so is what makes it survive review.

406
00:26:19.618 --> 00:26:24.734
Doing it out of order, above all lighting
up fast circuits before the security layer

407
00:26:24.734 --> 00:26:29.480
is live, is in the program's experience
the most common and most costly mistake.

408
00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:34.934
The boundary package carries the evidence:
the path policy, failover test records,

409
00:26:34.934 --> 00:26:38.783
path telemetry, each exception
with its need and duration,

410
00:26:38.783 --> 00:26:42.392
CISA parity, and the known
limitations stated openly.

411
00:26:42.392 --> 00:26:46.355
And when funding becomes possible,
prove the case in your own estate.

412
00:26:46.355 --> 00:26:51.780
Instrument two comparable branches for
30 days, change one, measure 30 more.

413
00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:56.342
The difference in differences is an effect
size a budget examiner will accept.

414
00:26:56.342 --> 00:26:58.935
The decision: adopt this order as program

415
00:26:58.935 --> 00:27:02.110
policy before the first
production cutover.

416
00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:06.785
Let me close with what can start now,
because most of it needs no award.

417
00:27:06.785 --> 00:27:10.196
The staff work: write
the application-to-path

418
00:27:10.196 --> 00:27:13.423
routing policy that
SC-7(4) already asks for.

419
00:27:13.423 --> 00:27:16.887
Its absence is the finding,
not the absence of a device,

420
00:27:16.887 --> 00:27:21.611
and it turns the eventual purchase
into enforcement of existing policy.

421
00:27:21.611 --> 00:27:25.435
Instrument a sample of branches
before the next migration wave.

422
00:27:25.435 --> 00:27:29.272
And verify that DNS egress
really ends at CISA.

423
00:27:29.272 --> 00:27:33.246
Before money moves: send
the target attributes to

424
00:27:33.246 --> 00:27:37.510
whoever scopes the BOD 26-02
replacements, in writing.

425
00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:42.635
Ask the field-office refresh request to
separate acquisition from five-year cost.

426
00:27:42.635 --> 00:27:47.148
And re-examine any ExpressRoute
arrangement for Microsoft 365;

427
00:27:47.148 --> 00:27:51.660
an approval granted years ago
isn't current Microsoft guidance.

428
00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:55.844
Before cutover: start CLAW coordination
with CISA and the provider,

429
00:27:55.844 --> 00:27:59.147
because it's a three-party
effort with its own lead time.

430
00:27:59.147 --> 00:28:03.709
Have the authorizing official record
the interim gap as an accepted risk.

431
00:28:03.709 --> 00:28:05.472
And name one decider per row.

432
00:28:05.472 --> 00:28:09.525
Recapping the seven decisions:
MTIPS ends on confirmed parity,

433
00:28:09.525 --> 00:28:14.200
the interim gap becomes an accepted
risk, the refresh carries the target,

434
00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:19.498
the investment order starts with staff
time, the field-office request gets tested,

435
00:28:19.498 --> 00:28:24.485
NPS retirement follows the Entra migration,
and the sequence becomes policy.

436
00:28:24.485 --> 00:28:25.885
One point on urgency.

437
00:28:25.885 --> 00:28:29.660
The branch measurement baseline
can't be reconstructed after the

438
00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:33.297
next migration wave, and it costs
staff time rather than capital.

439
00:28:33.297 --> 00:28:36.335
Buy transport on physics
and govern it as compliance,

440
00:28:36.335 --> 00:28:39.235
and the branch gets faster
without getting weaker.

441
00:28:39.235 --> 00:28:44.562
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
09 has the detail, every claim footnoted,

442
00:28:44.562 --> 00:28:49.272
and the backup slides list the sources
behind each slide in this briefing.

443
00:28:49.272 --> 00:28:50.090
Thank you.
